 See also `sample_limit` option at [scrape_config section](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs).
 
+## Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of scrape targets a single job (`job_name`) can have active at once.
+This means that if a service discovery source (Kubernetes, Consul, cloud APIs, a `file_sd_config` catalog, etc.)
+is misconfigured and suddenly returns an excessive number of targets for a job, `vmagent` tries to scrape all of
+them at once, which can spike memory and CPU usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same
+`vmagent` instance.
+
+The limit can be enforced in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied individually to every job
+  defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets_per_job` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_scrape_targets_per_job` option allows overriding the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+  If `max_scrape_targets_per_job` is set to `0` or left unset, then the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag value is used instead.
+
+The limit is applied uniformly regardless of which service discovery mechanism (or combination of `static_configs`,
+`file_sd_configs` and `*_sd_configs`) supplied the targets for the job. When a job's discovered target count exceeds
+its resolved limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to that number of targets for the job — it doesn't reject the
+whole job or crash. The excess targets are dropped with the `target_limit` drop reason and can be seen, together with
+the affected job's active/total target counts, at the `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` page (see
+[these docs](#scraping-big-number-of-targets)) and at the `/api/v1/targets?state=dropped` API endpoint.
+
 By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of time series written to remote storage systems specified at `-remoteWrite.url`.
 The limit can be enforced by setting the following command-line flags:
 
